The first and most obvious cause is the rapid increase in the number of private vehicles. As incomes rise, more families can afford cars and motorbikes, but road infrastructure has not developed at the same speed. Another cause is poor urban planning. In many cities, offices, schools and shopping centres are concentrated in the city centre, which means that millions of people travel to the same area at the same time every morning and evening. Finally, public transport in developing countries is often unreliable and uncomfortable, so people prefer to use their own vehicles.

Several measures could help to ease the situation. Governments should invest heavily in modern public transport systems, such as metro lines and bus rapid transit, and make them cheap and convenient enough to attract commuters. At the same time, policies like congestion charges or restrictions on vehicles entering the city centre during rush hour can discourage unnecessary journeys. In the long term, city planners should develop satellite towns with their own jobs and services, so that fewer people need to travel long distances.

In conclusion, traffic congestion is caused by the growing number of vehicles, poor planning and weak public transport. A combination of better public transport, smart traffic policies and balanced urban development is needed to solve this problem.', NULL, NULL, 250, DATEADD(minute,1290,CAST(DATEADD(day,-1,@today) AS datetime2(0))), 0, N'Submitted', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);

One major cause is urban migration. Young people move from rural areas to big cities in search of jobs and education, and the population of these cities grows faster than their roads can expand. In addition, private vehicles remain the most convenient choice for most commuters because public transport networks are limited. Many people also make short trips by car or motorbike that could easily be done on foot or by bicycle.

To address these issues, governments should first improve public transport. A reliable metro system with frequent services and integrated bus routes can carry thousands of passengers who would otherwise drive. Secondly, companies could be encouraged to adopt flexible working hours or allow employees to work from home several days a week, which would reduce the number of vehicles on the road during peak times. Thirdly, cities should create safe walking paths and cycling lanes to promote green transport for short distances.

To conclude, traffic congestion results from rapid urbanisation and the dominance of private vehicles. Investing in public transport, changing working patterns and promoting walking and cycling are effective ways to make city streets less crowded.', NULL, NULL, 232, DATEADD(minute,620,CAST(DATEADD(day,0,@today) AS datetime2(0))), 0, N'Submitted', NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL);
  Tr, Cc, Fc, Lr, Gra, P, OverallBand, AutoScore, AutoMaxScore, Feedback, FeedbackAudioUrl, ReturnNote, GradedAt, PublishedAt)
SELECT a.Id, s.Id, x.AttemptNo, x.Content, x.AudioUrl, x.AttachmentUrl, x.WordCount, x.SubmittedAt, x.IsLate, x.Status,
  x.Tr, x.Cc, x.Fc, x.Lr, x.Gra, x.P, x.Overall, x.AutoScore, x.AutoMax, x.Feedback, x.FeedbackAudioUrl, x.ReturnNote, x.GradedAt, x.PublishedAt
FROM #Sub x JOIN #A a ON a.K = x.A JOIN #S s ON s.No = x.No ORDER BY x.X;
CREATE TABLE #SM (X int PRIMARY KEY, SubId int);
JOIN Submissions sub ON sub.AssignmentId = a.Id AND sub.StudentId = s.Id AND sub.AttemptNo = x.AttemptNo;
CREATE TABLE #QA (X int, OrderNo int, Ans nvarchar(200) COLLATE DATABASE_DEFAULT);
UPDATE sub SET AnswersJson = (SELECT N'{' + STRING_AGG(CONCAT(N'"', qq.Id, N'":"', qa.Ans, N'"'), N',') WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY qq.OrderNo) + N'}'
    FROM #QA qa JOIN AssignmentQuestions qq ON qq.AssignmentId = sub.AssignmentId AND qq.OrderNo = qa.OrderNo WHERE qa.X = m.X)
FROM Submissions sub JOIN #SM m ON m.SubId = sub.Id WHERE EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM #QA WHERE X = m.X);
CREATE TABLE #AN (X int, Phrase nvarchar(500) COLLATE DATABASE_DEFAULT, ErrorType nvarchar(20) COLLATE DATABASE_DEFAULT, Suggestion nvarchar(1000) COLLATE DATABASE_DEFAULT, Comment nvarchar(1000) COLLATE DATABASE_DEFAULT);
SELECT m.SubId, p.Pos - 1, p.Pos - 1 + LEN(an.Phrase), an.Phrase, an.ErrorType, an.Suggestion, an.Comment
FROM #AN an JOIN #SM m ON m.X = an.X JOIN Submissions sub ON sub.Id = m.SubId
CROSS APPLY (SELECT CHARINDEX(an.Phrase, sub.Content) AS Pos) p WHERE p.Pos > 0;

